// The interpreter's global state and the questions asked of it before any
// evaluation happens: the mutable-array / string / hash stores, the TCP
// capability, the lexical scope chain, the (file, line) a string eval runs
// under, and the encoding tables (names, ASCII compatibility, validity,
// negotiation, and what Integer#chr needs to know).
//
// A MODULE, spliced by `import` where these chains used to sit. The TYPES stay
// in main.mere, ahead of every import, because every module needs them.
// ---- mutable-array store (M10) ------------------------------------------
// A single top-level Map captured by every function below (no threading):
// id (as str) -> the array's contents (a Val list). Arrays are handles
// (VArr id) into it, so aliases share one mutable list. A fresh id is
// map_len arr_store (arrays are never removed — memory grows, acceptable).

// ---- TCP (the `socket` capability) ---------------------------------------
// Mere's own byte-level socket FFI: the same externs mkv uses. Reads and
// writes cross through the shared byte arena (`mem_*`), so a payload with a
// zero byte survives. The Wasm host has no sockets and stubs these to -1.
extern fn tcp_connect: str -> int -> int;
extern fn tcp_listen: int -> int;
extern fn tcp_accept: int -> int;
extern fn tcp_read: int -> int -> int -> int;
extern fn tcp_write: int -> int -> int -> int;
extern fn tcp_close: int -> unit;
extern fn tcp_set_timeout: int -> int -> int;
extern fn mem_alloc: int -> int;
extern fn mem_to_str: int -> int -> str;
extern fn mem_copy_str: int -> int -> str -> int;
extern fn mem_set_u8: int -> int -> int -> int;
// libc's getpid: Process.pid has to be the real one -- a made-up number would
// be a stable lie, and sidekiq builds its identity string from it.
// libm, for the Math functions Mere has no builtin for. DERIVING them from
// the builtins does not work: every identity below is exact in real arithmetic
// and wrong in the last bit -- `exp (log x / 3.0)` answers 9.999999999999998
// for cbrt(1000), `atan2 x 1.0` differs from atan in the final digit, and
// `(e^x - e^-x) / (e^x + e^-x)` is NaN for tanh(1000) where the answer is 1.0.
// Byte-exactness against CRuby is the whole standard here, and CRuby calls
// these same functions.
extern fn atan: float -> float;
extern fn asin: float -> float;
extern fn acos: float -> float;
extern fn sinh: float -> float;
extern fn cosh: float -> float;
extern fn tanh: float -> float;
extern fn asinh: float -> float;
extern fn acosh: float -> float;
extern fn atanh: float -> float;
extern fn cbrt: float -> float;
extern fn log2: float -> float;
extern fn log10: float -> float;
// ruby 4.0 added Math.log1p / Math.expm1; libm has had them all along.
extern fn log1p: float -> float;
extern fn expm1: float -> float;
extern fn erf: float -> float;
extern fn erfc: float -> float;
extern fn tgamma: float -> float;
extern fn lgamma: float -> float;
extern fn hypot: float -> float -> float;
extern fn fmod: float -> float -> float;
extern fn ldexp: float -> int -> float;
extern fn getpid: unit -> int;
// chdir(2) / symlink(2): plain libc, and both answer an int -- an extern that
// returned `char*` would hand back a raw pointer rather than a Mere string
// (see the note at the top of this file), which is why realpath(3) is not
// here and File.realpath stays textual.
extern fn chdir: str -> int;
extern fn symlink: str -> str -> int;
// The rest of the write side, and the same rule decided which ones are here:
// Mere emits <stdio.h> and <unistd.h> and nothing else, so a libc function
// DECLARED in one of those can be called and one that is not cannot. Measured
// 2026-09-23 with the build's own clang: `rename` (stdio) and `truncate`,
// `link`, `readlink`, `chown` (unistd) compile; `chmod`, `umask` and `mkfifo`
// live in <sys/stat.h> and are a hard error -- "call to undeclared function",
// which `-w` does not silence because C99 made it an error, not a warning.
// Those three wait for a header, not for a design.
extern fn rename: str -> str -> int;
extern fn link: str -> str -> int;
// one arena block, reused: 64 KiB is the read size and the write chunk.

// Object ids come from a counter carried IN the ocls map itself (special key
// "$next", preserved by the collector's rebuild) -- not from map_len. With a
// collector dropping dead entries, map_len shrinks and a fresh object would
// collide with a survivor's id. The first call seeds from map_len, so a world
// built before any allocation behaves exactly as the old scheme did.
// ⚠ THE COUNTER LEFT THE CLASS TABLE because that table is keyed by an INT
// now, and "$next" is not one. It also stopped being a STRING: the id was
// written with str_of_int and read back with int_of_str on every object
// allocation. There is still exactly ONE allocator, which is what the note
// above is about -- the hazard was two of them disagreeing, and moving the
// counter out makes `map_len om` harder to mistake for the next id, not
// easier. `map_len om` stays as the fallback so the first id is what it
// always was.
let oid_next = map_new ();
// ⚠ The object->class and class->superclass tables, as GLOBALS. They are the
// third and second members of the `world` tuple and the driver builds exactly
// one world, so these ARE those maps -- named here so a world-free helper can
// read them. `class_name` is the reason: it answers the class in a refusal
// message and had no world to ask, so it said "Object" about every object in
// the program ("no implicit conversion of Object into Integer", for an M3).
let obj_cls = map_new ();
let cls_sup = map_new ();
// the class an object's REFUSAL MESSAGE names: its own, never the singleton
// that `def o.x` hung on it. (is_sng_cls lives in the eval chain, far below
// the two other askers, so the prefix test is written out here once.)
let obj_cls_name = fn (oid: int) ->
  let c = (if map_has obj_cls oid then map_get obj_cls oid else "Object") in
  if str_len c >= 5 && str_eq (substring c 0 5) "(sng:"
  then (if map_has cls_sup c then map_get cls_sup c else "Object") else c;
let alloc_oid = fn om ->
  let id = (if map_has oid_next "n" then map_get oid_next "n" else map_len om) in
  let _ = map_set oid_next "n" (id + 1) in
  id;
let arr_store = map_new ();
// Array ids come from a counter: the collector deletes dead entries now, so
// map_len shrinks and a length-derived id would collide with a survivor's.
// Slots, not names: every key here is a literal in this tree, and a string
// key costs a hash of the bytes plus a strcmp on each of the three probes
// `arr_new` makes -- once per Array the program builds.
//   0 n   1 flushes   2 hfast   3 hslow
let arr_ctr = map_new ();
let arr_new = fn (xs: Val list) ->
  let id = (if map_has arr_ctr 0 then map_get arr_ctr 0 else 0) in
  let _ = map_set arr_ctr 0 (id + 1) in
  let _ = map_set arr_store id xs in
  VArr id;
// ⚠ APPENDS GO INTO A Vec, NOT A LIST, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE POINT.
// An Array here is a `Val list`, and `push` was `arr_set id (list_append
// (deref id) args)` -- a copy of the entire array per element, so building one
// with `<<` was O(n^2): 32,000 pushes took 19.6 seconds where ruby takes 0.001.
//
// The first attempt queued the appends in another `Val list` and it did not
// help at all, which is the interesting part. A `list` is a VALUE type in
// Mere: `map_set` copies it into the MAP'S region, by design, so that the map
// outlives whatever region built the value (`__mcopy_list_V`). Queuing into a
// list still stored that list per push, so the copy just moved. Measured on
// its own: building a 32,000-element list is 0.37 ms, and storing it into a
// map on every step is 15,889 ms.
//
// A `Vec` is a REFERENCE type -- `__mcopy_Vec_V(r, v)` returns `v` -- so the
// map holds a HANDLE. Pushing through it never writes the map at all, and
// costs what a push should: 32,000 vec_pushes through a map_get is 0.09 ms.
// Nothing about Mere needed changing; the value/reference split IS the region
// model, and this was the wrong container on this side of it.
//
// `deref` folds the queue in before anyone sees the array, so all 338 readers
// are unchanged. The fold is one `list_append` over the SETTLED part, paid
// once per read after a run of appends -- and for the shape that matters,
// build-then-read (`n.times { a << i }`, and CSV.parse), the settled part is
// empty and the whole build is linear.
// ⚠ THE QUEUED VALUES ARE LIVE. gc_roots_check.sh is the gate that says so.
let arr_pending = map_new ();
let rec vec_tail_list = fn (v) -> fn (i: int) -> fn (acc: Val list) ->
  if i < 0 then acc else vec_tail_list v (i - 1) (Cons (vec_get v i, acc));
let deref = fn (id: int) ->
  // `map_len` first: nothing is queued in most programs, and then this costs
  // one comparison rather than a hash on every array read.
  if map_len arr_pending > 0 && map_has arr_pending id
  then
    (let v = map_get arr_pending id in
     let merged = list_append (map_get arr_store id) (vec_tail_list v (vec_len v - 1) (Nil : Val list)) in
     let _ = map_set arr_store id merged in
     let _ = map_delete arr_pending id in
     merged)
  else map_get arr_store id;
let arr_vers = map_new ();
let arr_ver = fn (id: int) -> (if map_has arr_vers id then map_get arr_vers id else 0);
// push without writing the map or the array: O(1) per element
let rec vec_push_all = fn (v) -> fn (xs: Val list) ->
  match xs with Nil -> () | Cons (h, t) -> (let _ = vec_push v h in vec_push_all v t);
let arr_push_many = fn (id: int) -> fn (xs: Val list) ->
  let v = (if map_has arr_pending id then map_get arr_pending id
           else (let nv = vec_new () in let _ = map_set arr_pending id nv in nv)) in
  let _ = map_set arr_vers id (arr_ver id + 1) in
  vec_push_all v xs;
let arr_set = fn (id: int) -> fn (xs: Val list) ->
  let _ = map_set arr_vers id (arr_ver id + 1) in
  // a whole-array replacement supersedes anything queued for it
  let _ = (if map_len arr_pending > 0 && map_has arr_pending id
           then (let _ = map_delete arr_pending id in ()) else ()) in
  let _ = map_set arr_store id xs in ();

// strings are mutable reference types too (Ruby semantics): VStr is a HANDLE
// into str_store, so aliases share one mutable string. Fresh id per literal
// evaluation — entries are never removed. A vec gives O(1) alloc/get/set
// (string allocation is far hotter than array allocation).
let str_store = vec_new ();
// ⚠ ONE SNAPSHOT PER SUBJECT, NOT PER MATCH. A MatchData keeps the subject it
// matched against (for #string / #pre_match / #post_match / $` / $'), and it
// has to be the text AS IT WAS -- ruby's MatchData holds a frozen copy. Making
// that copy per match is O(subject) memory that is never given back, so a lexer
// that matches once per token over one string used O(tokens x subject): parsing
// 3,200 quoted CSV rows peaked at 17.4 GB and 6,400 rows was killed. A scan
// almost always matches the SAME string over and over, so remember the last
// one: same bytes, same encoding, same snapshot. A mutation makes different
// bytes, so the check fails and a new snapshot is taken -- which is exactly the
// semantics a per-match copy had.
let rx_src_last = map_new ();
let rx_src_hnd = map_new ();
let mkstr = fn (s: str) ->
  let id = vec_len str_store in
  let _ = vec_push str_store s in
  VStr id;
let sget = fn (id: int) -> vec_get str_store id;
// ⚠ IS THIS STRING ALL ASCII? Cached per handle, because the answer decides
// whether a CHARACTER index is also a BYTE index -- and when it is, #length,
// #[] and matching from an offset are O(1) instead of a UTF-8 walk from the
// start of the string. Without it every one of those is O(n), so a lexer that
// advances through a subject pays O(n) per token and O(n^2) overall: CSV.parse
// on 1,600 quoted rows took 28 seconds, and the walk was 75% of the samples.
// The scan itself is O(n) and happens once per string.
let str_ascii = map_new ();
let rec str_all_ascii_go = fn (s: str) -> fn (i: int) -> fn (n: int) ->
  if i >= n then true
  else if ord (char_at s i) >= 128 then false
  else str_all_ascii_go s (i + 1) n;
let str_is_ascii = fn (hid: int) ->
  if map_has str_ascii hid then map_get str_ascii hid
  else (let a = (let v = sget hid in str_all_ascii_go v 0 (str_len v)) in
        let _ = map_set str_ascii hid a in a);
// the ONE place a stored string changes, so the one place the answer expires
let sset = fn (id: int) -> fn (s: str) ->
  let _ = map_delete str_ascii id in
  let _ = vec_set str_store id s in ();
// Kernel#gets state: stdin is slurped once (read_stdin) into stdin_src "s",
// then handed out one line at a time via a cursor stdin_src_pos "p". Lets
// `while line = gets` terminate at EOF (nil) the way Ruby does.
let stdin_src = map_new ();
let stdin_src_pos = map_new ();
// hashes are mutable reference types too: VHsh is a HANDLE into hash_store,
// which holds the interleaved [k1,v1,k2,v2,...] pair list. Aliases share one
// mutable hash (h[k]=v is seen through every reference).
let hash_store = vec_new ();
// The signal names a program may trap. mere-ruby never delivers one -- nothing
// here is a source of signals -- so this is a NAME table, used by Signal.list and
// Signal.signame, and the handlers a program registers live in signal_traps
// unread. A CLI traps INT before doing anything else, so answering matters more
// than refusing; the limit is in KNOWN_GAPS.
let signal_traps = map_new ();
let signal_names = "EXIT:0 IOT:6 EMT:7 CLD:20 HUP:1 INT:2 QUIT:3 ILL:4 TRAP:5 ABRT:6 FPE:8 KILL:9 BUS:10 SEGV:11 SYS:12 PIPE:13 ALRM:14 TERM:15 URG:16 STOP:17 TSTP:18 CONT:19 CHLD:20 TTIN:21 TTOU:22 IO:23 XCPU:24 XFSZ:25 VTALRM:26 PROF:27 WINCH:28 INFO:29 USR1:30 USR2:31";
// ⚠ AN INDEX, FOR THE KEYS WHOSE EQUALITY IS A VALUE COMPARISON.
// A Hash here is a flat assoc list, so `h[k]` walks it: measured, a read is
// `4.1 us + position x 14 ns`, and the walk overtakes the per-call constant at
// about position 290. For a 16,000-entry hash that is 112 us of scanning per
// read, and building one is 82% scan.
//
// The index is a digest -> value map beside the list. It is consulted ONLY
// when the hash is marked indexed, and the mark is dropped the moment anything
// happens that the index cannot describe. The safe direction is built in:
// `hkey_digest` answers "" for any key it does not know, and a hash holding
// one of those is not indexed at all -- a key kind added later is slow, never
// wrong. (Which is the opposite of a list of names that has to stay complete.)
//
// ⚠ Float, Rational, Complex and container keys are deliberately NOT here.
// `0` and `0.0` are different hash keys, `Complex(1,0)` and `1` are different
// again, and a container key is compared by its contents -- each is a rule
// that has to be got exactly right, and getting it wrong is a wrong answer
// rather than a slow one. They fall through to the scan.
let hash_index = map_new ();
let hash_indexed = map_new ();
// ⚠ `hderef` is defined below, after the tables this file declares in order,
// and a Hash used as a KEY has to be read whole -- including anything queued
// for it. A forward declaration is exactly the tool for that; it is the one
// the compiler grew today (v0.1.483) for cases like this.
let fn hderef_fwd: int -> Val list;
let rec hkey_digest = fn (k: Val) ->
  match k with
  | VInt n -> "i" ++ str_of_int n
  | VStr h -> "s" ++ sget h
  | VSym y -> "y" ++ y
  | VNil -> "n"
  | VBool b -> (if b then "t" else "f")
  // ⚠ TWO TRAPS IN A FLOAT KEY, both established by asking ruby:
  //   `0.0` and `-0.0` are ONE key -- but their printed forms differ, so a
  //   digest that spelled the number would make two. They compare equal, so
  //   the zero case is written once.
  //   NaN is NOT indexable. Ruby finds a NaN key by IDENTITY (`h[nan]` works
  //   for the object that was stored, and two distinct NaNs are two keys),
  //   and a content digest cannot tell them apart. `f == f` is false only for
  //   NaN, which is how it is recognised here.
  | VFlt f -> (if (if f == f then false else true) then ""
               else if f == 0.0 then "d0" else "d" ++ str_of_float f)
  // a Rational is stored normalised (Rational(2,4) prints "1/2"), so its two
  // parts identify it; a Complex is a pair, and each half must be digestible.
  | VRat (n, d) -> "r" ++ n ++ "/" ++ d
  | VCpx (a, b) ->
    (let da = hkey_digest a in let db = hkey_digest b in
     if str_eq da "" || str_eq db "" then ""
     else "c" ++ str_of_int (str_len da) ++ ":" ++ da ++ db)
  // ⚠ A CONTAINER KEY IS INDEXED ONLY NOW THAT ITS EQUALITY IS RIGHT. It was
  // left out while `{[1] => :x, [1.0] => :y}` was one entry here and two in
  // ruby -- an index over a wrong rule makes the wrong answer faster. hkey_eql
  // compares containers element-wise with eql? now, so a digest can mirror it.
  // ⚠ THE ENCODING HAS TO BE UNAMBIGUOUS. `[["a"], []]` and `[["a", []]]` are
  // different keys, so each element's digest is written with its LENGTH in
  // front: no separator can be confused with content.
  | VArr id -> hkey_seq "A" (deref id)
  | VHsh id -> hkey_seq "H" (hderef_fwd id)
  | _ -> ""
// "" if any element is not digestible -- the whole key then falls back to the
// scan, which is the safe direction.
and hkey_seq = fn (tag: str) -> fn (xs: Val list) ->
  let r = hkey_seq_go xs "" in
  if str_eq r (chr 1) then "" else tag ++ r
and hkey_seq_go = fn (xs: Val list) -> fn (acc: str) ->
  match xs with
  | Nil -> acc
  | Cons (h, t) ->
    (let d = hkey_digest h in
     if str_eq d "" then chr 1
     else
       (let rest = hkey_seq_go t (acc ++ str_of_int (str_len d) ++ ":" ++ d) in
        // a later element that is not digestible poisons the whole key
        if str_eq rest (chr 1) then chr 1 else rest));
// (the off-switch lives in main.mere, where env_get is in scope; this is the
//  plain question "does this hash have an index", asked while maintaining it)
let hidx_off = map_new ();
let hidx_on = fn (id: int) -> map_has hash_indexed id;
// anything the index cannot describe: the hash goes back to scanning
let hidx_drop = fn (id: int) ->
  if map_has hash_indexed id
  then (let _ = map_delete hash_indexed id in
        let _ = (if map_has hash_index id then (let _ = map_delete hash_index id in ()) else ()) in ())
  else ();
// a hash created EMPTY starts indexed; one created from pairs does not (the
// pairs would have to be walked and classified, and a literal is small)
let hidx_start = fn (id: int) ->
  let _ = map_set hash_indexed id true in
  let _ = map_set hash_index id (map_new ()) in ();
let hidx_put = fn (id: int) -> fn (d: str) -> fn (v: Val) ->
  map_set (map_get hash_index id) d v;
let hidx_has = fn (id: int) -> fn (d: str) -> map_has (map_get hash_index id) d;
let hidx_get = fn (id: int) -> fn (d: str) -> map_get (map_get hash_index id) d;
// build the index for a hash made from pairs, if EVERY key has a digest.
// One walk of the literal, and then `{a: 1}` filled in a loop is as fast as
// `{}` filled in a loop -- which it was not: 0.574 s against 0.010 s for the
// same 8,000 insertions, because starting from a literal left it unindexed
// for its whole life.
let rec hidx_seed = fn (id: int) -> fn (ys: Val list) ->
  match ys with
  | Nil -> true
  | Cons (k, Cons (v, t)) ->
    (let d = hkey_digest k in
     if str_eq d "" then false
     else (let _ = hidx_put id d v in hidx_seed id t))
  | _ -> false;
let mkhash = fn (xs: Val list) ->
  let id = vec_len hash_store in
  let _ = vec_push hash_store xs in
  let _ = (match xs with
           | Nil -> hidx_start id
           | _ -> (let _ = hidx_start id in
                   if hidx_seed id xs then () else hidx_drop id)) in
  VHsh id;
// ⚠ NEW PAIRS QUEUE IN A Vec, for the reason arr_pending does: `hset` is
// `vec_set hash_store id xs` and a `Val list` is a VALUE type, so storing it
// COPIES the whole hash into the Vec's region -- and `hash_put` rebuilt the
// list before that. Measured, 16,000 insertions: 11.3 s, of which the key
// SCAN is 1.07 s. The rebuild and the copy are the other 90%, and this is
// what removes them. A Vec is a reference type, so pushing through the map
// never writes the map.
// The queue holds k, v, k, v ... in insertion order, which is the order Ruby
// promises. `hderef` folds it in before anyone sees the hash, so all 145
// readers are unchanged.
// ⚠ What is left is the scan: finding whether the key is already there is
// still a walk, so building a hash is still quadratic -- about ten times
// cheaper, not linear. An index is the next change, not this one.
let hash_pending = map_new ();
let rec hvec_pairs = fn (v) -> fn (i: int) -> fn (acc: Val list) ->
  if i < 0 then acc else hvec_pairs v (i - 1) (Cons (vec_get v i, acc));
// a plain append, no dedup: only keys the caller has already found ABSENT are
// queued, so folding cannot introduce a duplicate. (`hash_put` and `hkey_eql`
// live in modules spliced after this one and are not reachable here -- which
// is also why the membership test below takes the comparison as an argument
// rather than writing the rule a second time.)
let hderef = fn (id: int) ->
  if map_len hash_pending > 0 && map_has hash_pending id
  then
    (let q = map_get hash_pending id in
     let merged = list_append (vec_get hash_store id) (hvec_pairs q (vec_len q - 1) (Nil : Val list)) in
     let _ = vec_set hash_store id merged in
     let _ = map_delete hash_pending id in
     merged)
  else vec_get hash_store id;
// the settled part WITHOUT folding the queue in -- the membership test that
// decides whether to queue must not flush the thing it is deciding about.
let hsettled = fn (id: int) -> vec_get hash_store id;
let hset = fn (id: int) -> fn (xs: Val list) ->
  // a whole-hash replacement supersedes anything queued for it -- and the
  // index cannot describe an arbitrary new list, so it goes too. This is the
  // single door for "the hash changed in a way this file did not choose",
  // which is what makes the index safe to believe when it is still there.
  let _ = (if map_len hash_pending > 0 && map_has hash_pending id
           then (let _ = map_delete hash_pending id in ()) else ()) in
  let _ = hidx_drop id in
  let _ = vec_set hash_store id xs in ();
let hderef_fwd = fn (id: int) -> hderef id;
// ⚠ A DELETE KEEPS THE INDEX, if the key has a digest. `hset` is the door for
// "the list changed in a way this file did not choose" and it drops the index;
// a delete is the one change that IS describable -- one key leaves -- so it
// gets its own door. Without it, one `h.delete` turned every later write in
// that hash back into a walk: 8,000 insertions after a single delete took
// 0.583 s against 0.010 s without it.
let hdel_indexed = fn (id: int) -> fn (k: Val) -> fn (xs: Val list) ->
  let keep = hidx_on id && (if str_eq (hkey_digest k) "" then false else true) in
  let _ = (if keep then (let _ = map_delete (map_get hash_index id) (hkey_digest k) in ()) else ()) in
  // hset would drop the index; write the list underneath it instead
  let _ = (if map_len hash_pending > 0 && map_has hash_pending id
           then (let _ = map_delete hash_pending id in ()) else ()) in
  let _ = (if keep then () else hidx_drop id) in
  let _ = vec_set hash_store id xs in ();
// queue a pair whose key is NOT already present (the caller has checked)
let hpush_new = fn (id: int) -> fn (k: Val) -> fn (v: Val) ->
  let q = (if map_has hash_pending id then map_get hash_pending id
           else (let nq = vec_new () in let _ = map_set hash_pending id nq in nq)) in
  // keep the index in step, or give it up: a key it cannot digest means the
  // index no longer knows everything in this hash.
  let _ = (if hidx_on id then
             (let d = hkey_digest k in
              if str_eq d "" then hidx_drop id else hidx_put id d v)
           else ()) in
  let _ = vec_push q k in vec_push q v;
// ⚠ THE MEMBERSHIP TEST IS NOT HERE. It needs `hkey_same`, which lives in a
// module spliced after this one -- and the first version passed the comparison
// in as a CLOSURE to get around that. It cost ten times per comparison what a
// direct call costs: the same 8,000,000 key comparisons are 0.081 s through
// `hash_haskey` and were 0.744 s through a closure parameter, which is the
// whole of what this change was supposed to save. The test lives beside its
// comparison now (hpending_has, main.mere) and this only hands out the queue.
let hpending_on = fn (id: int) -> map_len hash_pending > 0 && map_has hash_pending id;
let hpending_vec = fn (id: int) -> map_get hash_pending id;
// per-hash default value for missing keys (Hash.new(d)); keyed by handle id.
let hash_dflt = map_new ();
let hdflt_set = fn (id: int) -> fn (v: Val) -> let _ = map_set hash_dflt (str_of_int id) v in ();
let hdflt_get = fn (id: int) -> if map_has hash_dflt (str_of_int id) then map_get hash_dflt (str_of_int id) else VNil;
// Hash.new { |h, k| ... }: the block form of the default, kept as a proc
// handle per hash so a miss can call it with (hash, key).
let hash_dproc = map_new ();
let hdproc_set = fn (id: int) -> fn (v: Val) -> let _ = map_set hash_dproc (str_of_int id) v in ();
let hdproc_get = fn (id: int) -> if map_has hash_dproc (str_of_int id) then map_get hash_dproc (str_of_int id) else VNil;
// per-string encoding label (str handle id -> encoding name); default UTF-8.
let str_enc = map_new ();
