// ---- lexical scope chain ------------------------------------------------
// A locals env is a Map, but Ruby blocks nest scopes: a block's params and
// the locals it first assigns are block-local, while a name bound further
// out stays the SAME cell (assigning it inside the block is visible after).
// So a block body runs in a fresh child frame linked to its defining scope:
// lookup walks the chain, and assignment writes to the frame that already
// binds the name, otherwise declaring it in the innermost one. Because a
// proc/lambda captures the frame itself, the block-locals it closes over
// outlive the invocation — Ruby's closure semantics, which a single flat map
// with save/restore teardown could not express (the teardown deleted the
// very bindings the closure had captured).
// The ancestor chain of a class is asked for on EVERY dispatch -- `i + 1`
// asks whether Integer (then Numeric, then Object) redefines `+` -- and
// rebuilding it means splitting the include lists and appending fresh string
// lists each time. It changes only when someone includes, prepends, or names
// a superclass, so it is memoized against a counter those bump.
//
// MERE_RUBY_NO_MRO_CACHE=1 turns the memo off and recomputes every time: the
// old definition kept as an oracle, so the two can be compared over the whole
// corpus rather than trusted.
//   0 n (the generation)   1 off (MERE_RUBY_NO_MRO_CACHE, read once)
let mro_gen = map_new ();
let mro_anc = map_new ();      // cls -> its linearized ancestors
let mro_anc_gen = map_new ();  // cls -> the generation that list was built at
let mro_mods = map_new ();     // cls -> the modules included into it, expanded
let mro_mods_gen = map_new ();
let mro_bump = fn () ->
